<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WebmasterIDStoreKit — Apple privacy manifest.

  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  A SEPARATE MANIFEST, BECAUSE THIS PRODUCT IS SEPARATELY OPTIONAL
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Purchase History belongs to THIS module and to no other. Adding it to the
  core's manifest would make every app that uses WebmasterID for analytics
  declare that it collects purchase data — including apps that sell nothing and
  never link this product. The split in the package is mirrored here.

  ⚠ THE PREVIOUS BRANCH SHIPPED THIS MODULE WITH NO MANIFEST AT ALL, inheriting
  the core's by accident of layout. That was wrong in both directions: it
  under-declared Purchase History and it would have over-declared for a
  core-only consumer.

  WHAT THIS MODULE SENDS OFF-DEVICE
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  One JSON envelope per purchase: the Apple-signed JWS, the app property id, an
  opaque client-minted submission id, the consent state, and — only under
  `analyticsAllowed` — the account key the host application supplied. The
  server decodes the JWS and stores what Apple signed: product, price, currency,
  transaction identifiers.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT SEND, AND WHY EACH IS ABSENT
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DEVICE ID — not declared. The envelope has no installation identifier, no
  IDFV, no IDFA and no device field of any kind. The core declares Device ID
  because its EVENT envelope carries an installation id; this one does not.

  COARSE LOCATION — not declared, and this was checked against the receiving
  route rather than assumed. The core declares it because the events pipeline
  RETAINS an IP-derived country alongside the event. The StoreKit route resolves
  a client network identity for RATE LIMITING ONLY; it is not passed to the
  writer, and no App Store table has a country column. Nothing is retained, so
  nothing is declared.

  DIAGNOSTICS — not declared. The diagnostics this module exposes
  (`WebmasterIDStoreKitDiagnostics`) are counts and closed enum values, they
  stay on the device, and no code path transmits them. Apple's error text from a
  failed local verification is counted and immediately discarded, never stored
  and never sent.

  OTHER USAGE DATA — not declared. There is no free-form field in the envelope.

  PRODUCT INTERACTION — not declared HERE. A purchase is what Apple's
  "Purchase History" category is for; declaring both would describe the same
  bytes twice. The core declares Product Interaction for screen views and event
  names, which this module does not send.

  REQUIRED-REASON APIs — NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes IS EMPTY, AND AUDITED BY NAME
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This module touches FileManager (url(for:in:appropriateFor:create:),
  createDirectory, fileExists, removeItem), Data(contentsOf:), Data.write(to:
  options:), JSONEncoder/JSONDecoder, URLRequest and URLSession via the injected
  transport. None is on Apple's required-reason list.

  ⚠ FILE TIMESTAMPS ARE THE ONE WORTH NAMING. The evidence queue enforces an AGE
  bound, and the obvious implementation — reading the file's modification date —
  would be a required-reason API (category C617.1). It is not used: the age is
  measured from a `queuedAt` timestamp stored INSIDE each record. That was the
  correct behaviour independently, because the file is rewritten on every append
  and its mtime says when the queue last changed rather than how old the oldest
  purchase in it is. The privacy consequence is a bonus, not the reason.

  Also absent, each checked by name: UserDefaults, systemUptime,
  mach_absolute_time, volumeAvailableCapacity, statfs, and active keyboards.

  NSPrivacyTracking is false. No advertising identifier, no IDFV, no
  AppTrackingTransparency, no data broker, no cross-app or cross-site linking,
  and no tracking domains.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>

  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array>
    <!-- Apple's signed transaction: product, price, currency and the
         transaction identifiers the server decodes from the signature. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!-- The account key the host application passed to `identify`. Sent only
         under `analyticsAllowed`, refused outright if it looks like an email
         address, never written to this device's evidence queue, and hashed
         server-side before storage. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
